# The package delivered `/usr/libexec/opamp-fleet/supervisor` and nothing else. The install itself —
# the versioned layout, the `current` pointer, the systemd unit — belongs to the Client's own
# laid here — so `opamp-fleet --version` answers for the binary the service runs, not for whatever
# this package happened to deliver. The link carries the **product's** name and the file it resolves
# to carries the program's (ADR-0014 clause 3): two variant builds must not collide on one
# `/usr/bin` entry, and they would if the link were named after the program they share.
#
# The paths name the default system roots (`default_layout_root` and `default_root` in
# crates/client/src/service/manager.rs), the only roots a packaged install ever uses: this script
# calls plain `service install`. They are two roots on Linux — the executable layout under `/opt`,
# because SELinux never lets systemd execute a binary labeled `var_lib_t`; the configuration and
# state under `/var/lib`, where data belongs (ADR-0014 clause 8, carrying ADR-0014).
#
# There is no migration branch here, and that is ADR-0014 clause 10: no host was ever installed
# under the paths or names this release replaces, so there is nothing to carry across.
	systemctl is-active --quiet opamp-fleet.service; then
/usr/libexec/opamp-fleet/supervisor service install

ln -sfn /opt/opamp-fleet/current/supervisor /usr/bin/opamp-fleet
	systemctl restart opamp-fleet.service
  opamp-fleet service install --endpoint wss://<server>/v1/opamp
  systemctl start opamp-fleet
Run `opamp-fleet service install --interactive` instead to be asked for the credential too.
